Runtime Environments","Interpreters"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-1809","JavaScript engines (V8, SpiderMonkey)",["Runtime","Runtime Environments","Interpreters"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-1852","Native Execution",["Runtime","Runtime Environments"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-1877","Compiled languages that run directly on hardware",["Runtime","Runtime Environments","Native Execution"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-1931","Rust, C, C++, Go (when compiled to native code)",["Runtime","Runtime Environments","Native Execution"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-1984","Runtime Errors",["Runtime"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-2005","Errors that occur during program execution",["Runtime","Runtime Errors"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-2052","Examples:",["Runtime","Runtime Errors"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-2066","Null pointer exceptions",["Runtime","Runtime Errors","Examples:"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-2095","Array index out of bounds",["Runtime","Runtime Errors","Examples:"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-2126","Division by zero",["Runtime","Runtime Errors","Examples:"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-2148","File not found",["Runtime","Runtime Errors","Examples:"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-2168","Network timeouts",["Runtime","Runtime Errors","Examples:"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-2190","Contrast with compile-time errors, which prevent the program from being built",["Runtime","Runtime Errors"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-2272","Language Examples",["Runtime"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-2296","Dynamically Typed Languages",["Runtime","Language Examples"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-2332","Python, JavaScript, Ruby: Most type checking happens at runtime",["Runtime","Language Examples","Dynamically Typed Languages"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-2401","Type errors only discovered when code executes",["Runtime","Language Examples","Dynamically Typed Languages"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-2453","Statically Typed Languages",["Runtime","Language Examples"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-2488","Rust, Java, C#: Most type checking at compile-time",["Runtime","Language Examples","Statically Typed Languages"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-2544","Runtime errors typically from logic errors or resource issues",["Runtime","Language Examples","Statically Typed Languages"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-2611","Benefits and Trade-offs",["Runtime"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-2641","Flexibility: Can handle dynamic data and user input",["Runtime","Benefits and Trade-offs"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-2701","Runtime Optimization: Just-in-time (JIT) compilation can optimize hot paths",["Runtime","Benefits and Trade-offs"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-2785","Error Discovery: Some errors only appear at runtime",["Runtime","Benefits and Trade-offs"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-2845","Performance Overhead: Runtime checks add execution overhead",["Runtime","Benefits and Trade-offs"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-2913","Related",["Runtime"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-2927","Programming/Time/Compile - The compilation phase before execution",["Runtime","Related"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-3001","Runtime environments and virtual machines",["Runtime","Related"]],["p-df879350c3f21578.html","source-block-3047","Dynamic typing vs static typing",["Runtime","Related"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-0","Two's Complement",[]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-73","A mathematical operation on binary numbers and the most common method for representing signed integers in computing",["Two's Complement"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-192","Simplifies arithmetic operations and eliminates the issue of having two representations for zero",["Two's Complement"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-292","Overview",["Two's Complement"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-307","The most significant bit (MSB) indicates the sign of the number:",["Two's Complement","Overview"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-376","0 for positive numbers",["Two's Complement","Overview","The most significant bit (MSB) indicates the sign of the number:"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-406","1 for negative numbers",["Two's Complement","Overview","The most significant bit (MSB) indicates the sign of the number:"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-436","Positive numbers are represented as usual in binary",["Two's Complement","Overview"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-492","Negative numbers are represented by inverting all bits of their absolute value (one's complement) and adding one to the least significant bit (LSB)",["Two's Complement","Overview"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-644","Range",["Two's Complement"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-656","For an n-bit binary number:",["Two's Complement","Range"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-688","The range of representable integers is from -2^(n-1) to 2^(n-1) - 1",["Two's Complement","Range","For an n-bit binary number:"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-765","For example, an 8-bit system can represent integers from -128 to 127",["Two's Complement","Range","For an n-bit binary number:"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-843","Advantages",["Two's Complement"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-860","Single Zero Representation",["Two's Complement","Advantages"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-895","Unlike one's complement, which has both 00000000 and 11111111 representing zero, two's complement has a unique representation (00000000)",["Two's Complement","Advantages","Single Zero Representation"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-1043","Simplified Arithmetic",["Two's Complement","Advantages"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-1073","Addition, subtraction, and multiplication operations are the same for both signed and unsigned numbers",["Two's Complement","Advantages","Simplified Arithmetic"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-1181","Simplifies hardware design",["Two's Complement","Advantages","Simplified Arithmetic"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-1213","No need for separate signed and unsigned arithmetic circuits",["Two's Complement","Advantages","Simplified Arithmetic"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-1279","Converting to Two's Complement",["Two's Complement"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-1316","To find the two's complement of a binary number:",["Two's Complement","Converting to Two's Complement"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-1369","1. Invert all bits (one's complement)",["Two's Complement","Converting to Two's Complement","To find the two's complement of a binary number:"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-1412","2. Add one to the LSB",["Two's Complement","Converting to Two's Complement","To find the two's complement of a binary number:"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-1439","Example: Representing -5 in 8-bit",["Two's Complement","Converting to Two's Complement"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-1481","Start with the binary representation of 5: 00000101",["Two's Complement","Converting to Two's Complement","Example: Representing -5 in 8-bit"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-1542","Invert all bits: 11111010",["Two's Complement","Converting to Two's Complement","Example: Representing -5 in 8-bit"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-1575","Add one: 11111011",["Two's Complement","Converting to Two's Complement","Example: Representing -5 in 8-bit"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-1600","Thus, -5 is represented as 11111011 in 8-bit two's complement",["Two's Complement","Converting to Two's Complement","Example: Representing -5 in 8-bit"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-1671","Arithmetic Operations",["Two's Complement"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-1699","Addition",["Two's Complement","Arithmetic Operations"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-1716","Add the binary numbers directly",["Two's Complement","Arithmetic Operations","Addition"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-1753","If there's a carry out of the MSB, it's discarded",["Two's Complement","Arithmetic Operations","Addition"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-1808","Subtraction",["Two's Complement","Arithmetic Operations"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-1828","Convert the number to be subtracted into its two's complement",["Two's Complement","Arithmetic Operations","Subtraction"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-1895","Add it to the other number",["Two's Complement","Arithmetic Operations","Subtraction"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-1927","Example: Computing 5 - 3 in 4-bit",["Two's Complement","Arithmetic Operations"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-1969","Represent 5: 0101",["Two's Complement","Arithmetic Operations","Example: Computing 5 - 3 in 4-bit"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-1996","Represent 3: 0011",["Two's Complement","Arithmetic Operations","Example: Computing 5 - 3 in 4-bit"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-2023","Find two's complement of 3:",["Two's Complement","Arithmetic Operations","Example: Computing 5 - 3 in 4-bit"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-2058","Invert bits: 1100",["Arithmetic Operations","Example: Computing 5 - 3 in 4-bit","Find two's complement of 3:"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-2084","Add one: 1101",["Arithmetic Operations","Example: Computing 5 - 3 in 4-bit","Find two's complement of 3:"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-2106","Add 5 and -3:",["Two's Complement","Arithmetic Operations","Example: Computing 5 - 3 in 4-bit"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-2129","0101 + 1101 = 10010",["Arithmetic Operations","Example: Computing 5 - 3 in 4-bit","Add 5 and -3:"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-2161","Discard the carry: 0010 (which is 2 in decimal)",["Arithmetic Operations","Example: Computing 5 - 3 in 4-bit","Add 5 and -3:"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-2219","Sign Extension",["Two's Complement"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-2240","When increasing the number of bits (e.g., from 8-bit to 16-bit), the sign bit (MSB) is extended to preserve the number's value",["Two's Complement","Sign Extension"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-2371","Positive numbers: Pad with 0s",["Two's Complement","Sign Extension"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-2411","Negative numbers: Pad with 1s",["Two's Complement","Sign Extension"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-2451","Example",["Two's Complement","Sign Extension"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-2467","Extending -5 (11111011 in 8-bit) to 16-bit: 11111111 11111011",["Two's Complement","Sign Extension","Example"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-2540","Historical Context",["Two's Complement"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-2565","The concept of two's complement has been integral to computer arithmetic since the mid-20th century",["Two's Complement","Historical Context"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-2669","Notably utilized in early computers like the IBM System/360, introduced in 1964",["Two's Complement","Historical Context"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-2753","This contributed to its widespread adoption in the computing industry",["Two's Complement","Historical Context"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-2827","Related",["Two's Complement"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-2841","Binary number representation",["Two's Complement","Related"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-2874","Signed integer representation",["Two's Complement","Related"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-2908","Computer arithmetic",["Two's Complement","Related"]],["p-a2f42d60c436bd93.html","source-block-2932","Hardware design",["Two's Complement","Related"]],["p-baf37e93a285a138.html","source-block-0","values greater than the maximum value the type can hold “wrap around” to the minimum of the values the type can hold. In the case of a u8, the value 256 becomes 0, the value 257 becomes 1, and so on.",[]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-0","Zero-Based Indexing",[]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-78","Also known as: zero indexing, zero-based numbering",["Zero-Based Indexing"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-132","A fundamental concept in computer science and programming where the first element of a sequence (array, list, tuple, etc.) is assigned the index 0, the second element the index 1, and so on",["Zero-Based Indexing"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-325","Overview",["Zero-Based Indexing"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-340","The first element is at index 0",["Zero-Based Indexing","Overview"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-376","The second element is at index 1",["Zero-Based Indexing","Overview"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-413","The nth element is at index n-1",["Zero-Based Indexing","Overview"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-449","Contrasts with one-based indexing, where the first element is assigned the index 1",["Zero-Based Indexing","Overview"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-536","Rationale",["Zero-Based Indexing"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-552","Memory Address Calculation",["Zero-Based Indexing","Rationale"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-587","An array's name refers to the address of its first element",["Zero-Based Indexing","Rationale","Memory Address Calculation"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-651","The index represents an offset from this base address",["Zero-Based Indexing","Rationale","Memory Address Calculation"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-710","In C, the expression array[i] is equivalent to *(array + i)",["Zero-Based Indexing","Rationale","Memory Address Calculation"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-779","The element at index i is located at the memory address array plus i times the size of each element",["Zero-Based Indexing","Rationale","Memory Address Calculation"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-890","Starting the index at 0 simplifies this calculation, as the first element is at the base address with an offset of zero",["Zero-Based Indexing","Rationale","Memory Address Calculation"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-1015","Simplified Range Calculations",["Zero-Based Indexing","Rationale"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-1053","When defining ranges or performing calculations involving indices, zero-based indexing often leads to cleaner expressions",["Zero-Based Indexing","Rationale","Simplified Range Calculations"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-1180","The length of a sequence can be directly used as the upper bound in loops without additional adjustments",["Zero-Based Indexing","Rationale","Simplified Range Calculations"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-1290","Example: A loop from 0 to length-1 is more straightforward than from 1 to length",["Zero-Based Indexing","Rationale","Simplified Range Calculations"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-1376","Advantages",["Zero-Based Indexing"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-1393","Simplified Calculations: Cleaner and more intuitive expressions for ranges and loops",["Zero-Based Indexing","Advantages"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-1486","Alignment with Memory Addressing: Aligns with the way memory addresses are computed, leading to more efficient pointer arithmetic",["Zero-Based Indexing","Advantages"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-1624","Widespread Adoption: Used by most modern programming languages",["Zero-Based Indexing","Advantages"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-1695","Programming Languages Using Zero-Based Indexing",["Zero-Based Indexing"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-1749","C",["Zero-Based Indexing","Programming Languages Using Zero-Based Indexing"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-1755","C++",["Zero-Based Indexing","Programming Languages Using Zero-Based Indexing"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-1763","Java",["Zero-Based Indexing","Programming Languages Using Zero-Based Indexing"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-1772","Python",["Zero-Based Indexing","Programming Languages Using Zero-Based Indexing"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-1783","JavaScript",["Zero-Based Indexing","Programming Languages Using Zero-Based Indexing"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-1798","Go",["Zero-Based Indexing","Programming Languages Using Zero-Based Indexing"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-1805","Swift",["Zero-Based Indexing","Programming Languages Using Zero-Based Indexing"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-1815","Kotlin",["Zero-Based Indexing","Programming Languages Using Zero-Based Indexing"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-1826","Rust",["Zero-Based Indexing","Programming Languages Using Zero-Based Indexing"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-1835","Contrast with One-Based Indexing",["Zero-Based Indexing"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-1874","Some languages, particularly those used in scientific computing, prefer one-based indexing to align with traditional mathematical notation",["Zero-Based Indexing","Contrast with One-Based Indexing"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-2017","Examples:",["Zero-Based Indexing","Contrast with One-Based Indexing"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-2031","Fortran",["Zero-Based Indexing","Contrast with One-Based Indexing","Examples:"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-2044","MATLAB",["Zero-Based Indexing","Contrast with One-Based Indexing","Examples:"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-2056","R",["Zero-Based Indexing","Contrast with One-Based Indexing","Examples:"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-2063","In these languages, the first element of a sequence is accessed with index 1",["Zero-Based Indexing","Contrast with One-Based Indexing"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-2144","Can be more intuitive for users familiar with mathematical conventions",["Zero-Based Indexing","Contrast with One-Based Indexing"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-2219","Examples",["Zero-Based Indexing"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-2234","Zero-Based (Most Languages)",["Zero-Based Indexing","Examples"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-2270","First element: array[0]",["Zero-Based Indexing","Examples","Zero-Based (Most Languages)"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-2301","Second element: array[1]",["Zero-Based Indexing","Examples","Zero-Based (Most Languages)"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-2333","Last element of n-length array: array[n-1]",["Zero-Based Indexing","Examples","Zero-Based (Most Languages)"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-2383","One-Based (Some Languages)",["Zero-Based Indexing","Examples"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-2418","First element: array[1]",["Zero-Based Indexing","Examples","One-Based (Some Languages)"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-2449","Second element: array[2]",["Zero-Based Indexing","Examples","One-Based (Some Languages)"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-2481","Last element of n-length array: array[n]",["Zero-Based Indexing","Examples","One-Based (Some Languages)"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-2529","Related",["Zero-Based Indexing"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-2543","Array indexing and access",["Zero-Based Indexing","Related"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-2573","Memory addressing and pointer arithmetic",["Zero-Based Indexing","Related"]],["p-f7f2678dd499987b.html","source-block-2618","Programming language design",["Zero-Based Indexing","Related"]],["p-aa746e51d86626a4.html","source-block-99","Project - in",[]],["p-aa746e51d86626a4.html","source-block-176","Person/codekiln/GitHub/codekiln/Issue/01 increase resolution of life and package it up",["Project - in"]],["p-aa746e51d86626a4.html","block-67c04f1a-4aaa-4a4e-ace8-5e8901102982","Summary",["Project - in","Person/codekiln/GitHub/codekiln/Issue/01 increase resolution of life and package it up"]]],"format_version":2}